; ======================================================================
;  hwtimer.pi3 - the Raspberry Pi 3's implementation of THE TIME seam.
;
;  The seam is declared in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi under THE TIME: Ticks,
;  TickHz, TimerInit, Micros, millis, delayMicroseconds, delay and
;  HwMicros. Anvil/Core/wallclock.pbi anchors UTC to it,
;  Anvil/Storage/hwfile.pbi stamps every file it writes with the result,
;  Anvil/Core/netconsole.pbi paces its datagrams with it, and
;  Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi hands a payload the microseconds. None of them names
;  a chip.
;
;  IT IMPLEMENTS THE DECLARED SEAM AND ADDS NO CONVENTION OF ITS OWN.
;  That is the whole reason this file exists rather than the board simply
;  calling Pi3Micros() where it needs a time: the tree already had the
;  same generic-timer read in twenty-odd private copies under twenty-odd
;  private names, and they had begun to diverge. This board's own
;  RaspberryPi3/Lib/timer.pbi is one of them - Pi3Micros and Pi3DelayUs -
;  and it stays exactly as it is, because the immutable loader is built
;  from it and it is proven on silicon. What changes is that nothing
;  ABOVE this file has to know those names.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE Pi 3 IS SIMPLER THAN EVERY OTHER BOARD
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The BCM2837's system timer is a free-running 64-bit counter at exactly
;  1 MHz - the peripherals document says so rather than leaving it to be
;  measured. So a tick IS a microsecond, TickHz() is a constant that
;  happens to be true, and every conversion below is either nothing at
;  all or a divide by a thousand.
;
;  The other boards read the AArch64 generic timer - 54 MHz on one part,
;  19.2 MHz on another - and must convert, with the overflow the seam's
;  declaration warns about: ticks * 1000000 overflows a signed 64-bit
;  value after about fifteen minutes at 19.2 MHz, which is exactly long
;  enough for a bench session to look fine. There is nothing here to get
;  wrong and nothing to measure.
;
;  IT IS A CONSTANT AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, which is worth one sentence: if
;  a future part in this family clocked its system timer differently, this
;  number would be wrong and would look right. What guards against that is
;  the target - this file only ever compiles for BCM2837 - and not a
;  run-time check, because there is no register that reports the system
;  timer's rate to check a constant against.
;
;  WHY NOT THE GENERIC TIMER, which this part also has. Two reasons and
;  the second is the one that decides it. CNTFRQ_EL0 on a Pi 3 is whatever
;  the firmware wrote into it and is a value this monitor would have to
;  trust rather than know. And the system timer is ALREADY RUNNING AND
;  ALREADY TRUSTED here: the immutable loader times its three-second
;  recovery window on it, the SD host times its transfers on it, and the
;  never-fed watchdog this monitor has fifteen seconds to release is
;  measured against the same clock. Using a different counter for the
;  monitor's own time than the one the boot chain used would put two
;  clocks in one boot for no gain.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DEPENDENCIES: RaspberryPi3/Lib/timer.pbi (Pi3Micros) must be included
;  before this file. House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Ticks() - the raw monotonic count. Microseconds, here.
;
;  NEVER BACKWARDS AND NEVER NEGATIVE. Pi3Micros() reads the counter's
;  high word twice and retries if it changed between them, so a read that
;  straddles the low word wrapping is discarded rather than returned as a
;  number that jumps by an hour. It answers -1 when eight attempts all
;  straddled, which cannot happen with a running timer and would mean the
;  timer has stopped; the seam says a reader that cannot get a coherent
;  value answers 0, so that is what happens to the -1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Ticks()
  Define t.i
  t = Pi3Micros()
  If t < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; One tick is one microsecond on this part. See the header for why this
; is a constant and what would make it a lie.
Procedure.i TickHz()
  ProcedureReturn 1000000
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  TimerInit() - nothing to do, and saying so beats an empty procedure.
;
;  The system timer is running before the firmware hands the ARM over.
;  Every early diagnostic in this tree reads it without preparing it, and
;  the immutable loader's recovery window is timed by it before this
;  monitor exists. There is no enable bit, no clock gate and no divisor
;  to program, so the honest answer is 1 rather than a procedure that
;  looks like it did something.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i TimerInit()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Micros() - microseconds since the counter's origin.
;
;  NO CONVERSION AT ALL, because a tick is a microsecond here. On the
;  other boards this is a divide with an overflow trap in it; here the
;  only thing to get right is the stopped-clock case, and Ticks() has
;  already turned that into a 0.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Micros()
  ProcedureReturn Ticks()
EndProcedure

; Milliseconds. One exact divide; 1000000 / 1000 leaves no remainder to
; round away.
Procedure.i millis()
  ProcedureReturn Ticks() / 1000
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  delayMicroseconds(us) - wait, measured against the counter.
;
;  1 if the wait completed, 0 if it gave up because the counter was not
;  advancing. A ZERO OR NEGATIVE ARGUMENT RETURNS 0, which is the seam's
;  contract and not this board's opinion.
;
;  IT DOES NOT CALL Pi3DelayUs(), and that is deliberate rather than
;  wasteful. That procedure refuses anything above one second, which is
;  right for the early diagnostics it was written for and wrong for a
;  general wait; wrapping it would mean a caller asking for two seconds
;  getting an immediate 0 that looks like a stopped clock.
;
;  THE BOUND IS A REAL ONE AND IT IS NOT THE DEADLINE. The loop gives up
;  after a fixed number of reads as well as at the deadline, so a counter
;  that has stopped is detected by the count rather than by waiting for a
;  deadline that can never arrive. A monitor that spun for ever inside a
;  wait would be reset by the loader's watchdog with nothing printed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i delayMicroseconds(us.i)
  Define start.i
  Define now.i
  Define n.i

  If us <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  start = Pi3Micros()
  If start < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0                  ; no clock - do not pretend to wait
  EndIf
  For n = 0 To 99999999
    now = Pi3Micros()
    If now < 0 Or now < start
      ProcedureReturn 0                ; it stopped, or went backwards
    EndIf
    If now - start >= us
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  delay(ms) - milliseconds.
;
;  IT ANSWERS 1 FOR A ZERO ARGUMENT WHILE delayMicroseconds(0) ANSWERS 0.
;  That reads like a defect and the seam's declaration says it is a
;  CONTRACT: both names are called across the fleet and the pair behaves
;  this way on every part that has them. Copied rather than tidied. Fix
;  it in every implementation or in none.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i delay(ms.i)
  If ms <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn delayMicroseconds(ms * 1000)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMicros() - the same microseconds, under the name the payload ABI
;  asks a board for.
;
;  IT IS Micros() AND NOT A SECOND READ. Two entry points that each read
;  the counter their own way is how a payload and the monitor end up
;  disagreeing about what time it is; there is one source here and this
;  is a name for it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMicros()
  ProcedureReturn Micros()
EndProcedure
